  "content": {
    "title": "NCAA basketball tournament Selection Sunday start time, where to watch: Who makes March Madness?",
    "text": "Updated March 15, 2026, 5:43 p.m. ETThe 68-team field includes 31 conference tournament champions and 37 at-large teams picked by a selection committee.The tournament concludes April 6 with the national championship game at Lucas Oil Stadium in Indianapolis.The selection show is at 6 p.m. ET Sunday, March 15, on CBS and Paramount+.The 68-team field for the 2026 NCAA Tournament will be revealed Sunday, March 15. Thirty-one teams earn tournament bids by winning conference tournaments, while 37 at-large teams are picked by a selection committee.The tournament begins Tuesday, March 17, with the First Four in Dayton, Ohio. March Madness ends with the Final Four at Lucas Oil Stadium in Indianapolis on April 4 and 6.What day is Selection Sunday 2026? Start time for Selection Sunday 20266 p.m. ET Sunday, March 15What channel is Selection Sunday 2026 on? How can I watch Selection Sunday 2026?TV, streaming: CBS, Paramount+Watch March Madness with a free Fubo trialWhere is the Final Four 2026?Lucas Oil Stadium in IndianapolisWho will win March Madness in 2026?The nation's highest-ranked teams heading toward the NCAA Tournament include Duke, Michigan, Arizona, Iowa State, Connecticut, Florida, and Houston.When is March Madness 2026? NCAA Tournament schedule 2026First Four: March 17-18, Tuesday-Wednesday, at Dayton, OhioFirst round: March 19-20, Thursday-Friday, at Buffalo, New York; Greenville, South Carolina; Oklahoma City; Portland, Oregon; Tampa, Florida; Philadelphia; San Diego; St. Louis.Second round: March 21-22, Saturday, Sunday, at Buffalo, New York; Greenville, South Carolina; Oklahoma City; Portland, Oregon; Tampa, Florida; Philadelphia; San Diego; St. Louis.Sweet 16: March 26-27, Thursday, Friday, at Houston (South); San Jose, California (West); Chicago (Midwest); Washington (East)Elite Eight: March 28-29, Saturday, Sunday, at Houston (South); San Jose, California (West); Chicago (Midwest); Washington (East)Final Four: Saturday, April 4, at Lucas Oil Stadium in IndianapolisNational championship game: Monday, April 6, Lucas Oil Stadium in IndianapolisWhere will NCAA Tournament games be played in 2026? Who is hosting March Madness 2026 games?First Four: Dayton, OhioFirst and second rounds: Buffalo, New York; Greenville, South Carolina; Oklahoma City; Portland, Oregon; Tampa, Florida.; Philadelphia; San Diego; St. Louis.Regionals: Houston (South); San Jose, California (West); Chicago (Midwest); Washington (East)Final Four: Lucas Oil Stadium in IndianapolisHow much are NCAA Tournament tickets? How to get March Madness ticketsBuy Purdue basketball March Madness tickets!Who won the 2025 national championship? Who won March Madness in 2025?Florida.Who is in the 2026 NCAA Tournament? Who has earned automatic bids to March Madness 2026?Atlantic Coast (ACC): DukeAmerica East: UMBCAmerican: South FloridaAtlantic Sun (ASun): QueensAtlantic 10 (A-10): VCUBig 12: ArizonaBig East: St. John'sBig Sky: IdahoBig South: High PointBig Ten: PurdueBig West: HawaiiCoastal (CAA): HofstraConference USA (C-USA): Kennesaw StateHorizon: Wright StateIvy: PennMid-Atlantic (MAAC): SienaMid-American (MAC): AkronMid-Eastern (MEAC): HowardMountain West: Utah StateMissouri Valley (MVC): Northern IowaNortheast (NEC): Long IslandOhio Valley (OVC): Tennessee StatePatriot: LehighSoutheastern (SEC):Southern (SoCon): FurmanSouthland: McNeese StateSummit: North Dakota StateSun Belt: TroySouthwestern (SWAC): Prairie View A&MWestern (WAC): Cal BaptistWest Coast (WCC): GonzagaWill Miami (Ohio) make March Madness? Who is on the bubble for March Madness? Potential at-large teams for the 2026 NCAA TournamentMichigan, Florida, Illinois, Houston, Iowa State, Connecticut, Michigan State, Nebraska, Louisville, Virginia, Texas Tech, Vanderbilt, Alabama, Arkansas, Auburn, Kansas, Tennessee, Saint Mary's, North Carolina, BYU, Iowa, Wisconsin, Saint Louis, Kentucky, Ohio State, Georgia, Miami (Fla.), Miami (Ohio), Villanova, UCLA, North Carolina State, Clemson, SMU, Santa Clara, TCU, Texas, Texas A&M, Missouri, VCU, UCF, Oklahoma, New Mexico, San Diego State.",
